nodecode-cacheguard is a rewrite of AIverflow's AIverflow/hermes-cacheguard:

  https://github.com/AIverflow/hermes-cacheguard
  commit 4648bc056c28b9453d92089ffd9db09f11dc3392
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 AIverflow (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the whole guard, in Common Lisp, inside Nodecode's own
seams. The original is a Hermes plugin that registers two LLM middlewares and
two hooks (a pre-call veto and a post-call result line). Here one function is
advised — the call Nodecode makes to its provider, once per round — so the
pause clock, the prompt size, the block and the result line all come from the
same place: a saved call time and prompt size per session, the usage the round
brings back, and a small journal of pauses. The block is the round's answer
rather than a withheld message: the provider is never called, the operator
reads the BLOCKED line, and sending again goes out on a restarted clock. The
settings are one declared section, and /cacheguard reports and changes them
(in memory, and in the shared config where it can be written).

What was left out and why: the per-surface groups (cli, desktop, gateway) and
the per-model overrides are one mode and one pair of thresholds here, because a
Nodecode cell has one section and Nodecode does not tell a provider call which
surface it came from. The learned journal, the "lost 4 of 10 similar pauses"
line and the block history are live-only: they are counts kept in memory, so a
restart begins empty rather than reading the original's observations file. The
result line is a Nodecode notice, not a message appended to the transcript,
because a cell does not write into a session's log. Skipped too: the
tiny_block short form, the image special case, the restarted-gateway advice,
the compatibility scripts and the image handling of the original repository.
